    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326606": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Professor",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Research Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Research Assistant Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Information Posting Information Department Psychiatry - Research - 412250 Posting Open Date 08/06/2026 Application Deadline Open Until Filled Yes Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Research Assistant Professor Appointment Type Faculty Vacancy ID FAC0006044 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Dependent on Experience and Qualifications Proposed Start Date 10/01/2026 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! A global higher education leader in innovative teaching, research and public service, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill consistently ranks as one of the nation’s top public universities . Known for its beautiful campus, world-class medical care, commitment to the arts and top athletic programs, Carolina is an ideal place to teach, work and learn. One of the best college towns and best places to live in the United States, Chapel Hill has diverse social, cultural, recreation and professional opportunities that span the campus and community. University employees can choose from a wide range of professional training opportunities for career growth, skill development and lifelong learning and enjoy exclusive perks for numerous retail, restaurant and performing arts discounts, savings on local child care centers and special rates on select campus events. UNC -Chapel Hill offers full-time employees a comprehensive benefits package , paid leave, and a variety of health, life and retirement plans and additional programs that support a healthy work/life balance. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit Our mission is to improve the health and well-being of North Carolinians and others whom we serve. We accomplish this by providing leadership and excellence in the interrelated areas of patient care, education and research. Patient care: We promote health and provide superb clinical care while maintaining our strong tradition of reaching underserved populations and reducing health disparities across North Carolina and beyond. Education: We prepare tomorrow’s healthcare professionals and biomedical researchers from all backgrounds by facilitating learning within innovative and integrated curricula and team-oriented interprofessional education to ensure a highly skilled workforce. Research: We develop and support a rich array of outstanding health sciences research programs, centers and resources. We provide infrastructure and opportunities for collaboration among disciplines throughout and beyond our university to support outstanding research. We foster programs in the areas of basic, translational, mechanistic and population research. Position Summary The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill Center of Excellence for Eating Disorders ( UNC CEED ), established in 2003, is seeking candidates for a fixed-term Research Assistant Professor position in the Department of Psychiatry. The candidate will dedicate the majority of their time to research, specifically pursuing an active program of externally funded research (K- or R- level or equivalent) and actively and effectively pursuing and achieving publication in appropriate scholarly venues. Additionally, the successful candidate will offer a rich training environment for PhD students and post-doctoral fellows along with training and supervision of behavioral health trainees (including clinical psychology interns and psychiatry residents). This role also includes lectures to medical students and trainees in psychology, psychiatry, pediatrics, and other specialties. They will participate in the CEED Summer Research Fellow program, mentoring promising junior investigators in the field. Clinical responsibilities may include assessment and treatment services to children and families in inpatient and outpatient settings. We are seeking a candidate to contribute to the future vision and direction of CEED , who is passionate about advancing the science of eating disorders. CEED is world-renowned for excellence in research, treatment, and training in eating disorders. Our research goals are to develop a deeper understanding of the causes of eating disorders and to create more effective diagnostic, treatment, and prevention strategies. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements Applicants must have a Ph.D. and/or M.D. (or equivalent) in a relevant field and must be licensed to practice in NC (or be license eligible). Experience in both research and treatment of eating disorders is required. Evidence of external funding at R-level or the equivalent is essential. Candidates must have a programmatic line of research as evidenced via scholarship and research productivity referenced on their CV. Prior history of teaching, supervising, or mentoring advanced trainees is required. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Not applicable. Special Physical/Mental Requirements Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/323507 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Ammie Senn, HR Consultant Department Contact Telephone or Email ammie_senn@med.unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter List of References Research Statement Optional Documents Teaching Philosophy/evaluations Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What is your highest degree? PhD MD PharmD DDS DVM JD MLS/MIS Masters Other * Have you been involved in performing research related to eating disorders or body image? Yes No * Do you have evidence of and potential for attracting external funding at R-level or the equivalent? YES NO * What experience do you have with teaching and/or mentoring learners in conducting research? What topics are you most comfortable with teaching? (Open Ended Question) * Have you been the primary author, including writing of the initial draft, of a peer reviewed scientific publication? (Open Ended Question) Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326564": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Artificial Intelligence - Writing, Editing, and Digital Publishing",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Assistant Professor of Artificial Intelligence - Writing, Editing, and Digital Publishing Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Assistant Professor of Artificial Intelligence - Writing, Editing, and Digital Publishing Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications should include the following materials: A cover letter discussing the applicant’s teaching, research, and interest in the position; A curriculum vitae; A research statement describing current and future scholarly work; A writing sample; A syllabus for a course related to writing, editing, digital publishing, rhetoric, or AI and writing, accompanied by a brief contextual statement explaining how the course aligns with the applicant’s pedagogical philosophy; and Four letters of recommendation. Please upload all required application materials through the application portal. For questions related to the search, please contact the Search Committee Chair, David Baker, at davidbak@email.unc.edu. For technical assistance or questions regarding the application portal, please contact Sujata Paudel at paudels@unc.edu. Posting Information Posting Information Department English and Comp Literature - 311500 Posting Open Date 10/06/2026 Application Deadline 11/16/2026 Open Until Filled No Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Assistant Professor of Artificial Intelligence - Writing, Editing, and Digital Publishing Appointment Type Tenured/Tenure Track Vacancy ID FAC0006149 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Dependent on Experience and/or Qualifications Proposed Start Date 07/01/2027 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! The Department of English and Comparative Literature at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor in Writing, Editing, and Digital Publishing, beginning July 1, 2027. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Rhetoric and Composition, English, Writing Studies, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. We seek a teacher-scholar whose research and teaching engage the evolving relationships among writing, editing, digital publishing, and artificial intelligence. We welcome candidates working across rhetoric and composition, writing studies, technical and professional communication, digital rhetoric, literacy studies, or related fields. The successful candidate will maintain a research program that examines how AI is reshaping writing, editing, digital publishing, and rhetoric. We are particularly interested in scholars who move beyond simple narratives of technological optimism or critique. We seek candidates who approach artificial intelligence as a complex scholarly problem that demands historical, theoretical, empirical, creative, and pedagogical investigation. Candidates should demonstrate how their work develops and applies questions about authorship, creativity, expertise, communication, labor, literacy, ethics, publishing, and/or the future of humanistic inquiry. Candidates should also demonstrate a record of research, publication, and practical and pedagogical engagement with emerging technologies. Candidates should demonstrate the ability to work across disciplinary boundaries through expertise in multiple areas of study and the creative integration of diverse approaches. We also seek candidates with a commitment to collaborative scholarship, which may be demonstrated through leadership and participation in academic programs, research initiatives, or public-facing projects. Beyond research collaborations, we value public outreach, the sharing of resources, and community-building activities that contribute to vibrant intellectual communities and broaden the impact of scholarly work. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit The Department of English and Comparative Literature is committed to hiring an exceptional teacher/scholar who brings energy and a fresh perspective to their research, pedagogy, and service and who is ready to teach in our Rhetoric and Composition program. We seek candidates who can contribute to UNC’s academic mission as a public institution, especially through commitment to diversity of perspective, intercultural understanding, and engagement with local resources and communities. We encourage candidates to consult our website to learn about the Department’s curriculum, values, and mission. Candidates working across disciplines are encouraged to apply. The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill is the flagship institution of a strong state university system comprising 17 campuses across North Carolina; neighbor institutions within 25 miles include Duke University, North Carolina Central University, North Carolina State University, and Meredith College. Chapel Hill is a vibrant college town near the Research Triangle economic hub and 20 minutes from Raleigh-Durham International Airport. Position Summary The Department of English and Comparative Literature at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill is recruiting an Assistant Professor of Artificial Intelligence – Writing, Editing, and Digital Publishing. The successful candidate’s duties will include teaching, research, and service. This Assistant Professor will teach a 2-2 load made up of graduate and undergraduate courses; contribute to both undergraduate and graduate curricula; mentor students; participate in select administrative activities; and engage in ongoing scholarly research. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements Ph.D. English, Rhetoric and Composition, or a related degree and instructional experience at the college level by the time of appointment. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Demonstrated excellence in college-level teaching, a strong research portfolio, expertise in research methods, a history of collaboration, experience in research and teaching related to Artificial Intelligence and its relationship to Writing, Editing, and Digital Publishing. Special Physical/Mental Requirements N/A. Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions Applications should include the following materials: A cover letter discussing the applicant’s teaching, research, and interest in the position; A curriculum vitae; A research statement describing current and future scholarly work; A writing sample; A syllabus for a course related to writing, editing, digital publishing, rhetoric, or AI and writing, accompanied by a brief contextual statement explaining how the course aligns with the applicant’s pedagogical philosophy; and Four letters of recommendation. Please upload all required application materials through the application portal. For questions related to the search, please contact the Search Committee Chair, David Baker, at davidbak@email.unc.edu. For technical assistance or questions regarding the application portal, please contact Sujata Paudel at paudels@unc.edu. Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326415 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title David J. Baker/ Phialas Distinguished Professor Department Contact Telephone or Email davidbak@email.unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter List of References Writing/research Sample Sample Syllabus Research Statement Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What is your highest degree? PhD MD PharmD DDS DVM JD MLS/MIS Masters Other Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326559": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Assistant Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. HOW TO APPLY: Qualified persons should submit all application materials: 1. Cover letter including descriptions of your current and future research, as well as your teaching experience and philosophy. 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. The names and contact information of FOUR references should be appended to the cover letter. (References will be contacted by the search committee when the short list of applicants is being determined.) Search Chair: Dr. Benjamin Waterhouse (waterhou@email.unc.edu) Posting Information Posting Information Department History - 315900 Posting Open Date 09/25/2026 Application Deadline 11/02/2026 Open Until Filled No Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Assistant Professor Appointment Type Tenured/Tenure Track Vacancy ID FAC0006111 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Dependent on Experience and/or Qualifications Proposed Start Date 07/01/2027 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! Please direct questions to the chair of the search committee, Dr. Benjamin Waterhouse (waterhou@email.unc.edu). Areas of teaching responsibility include introductory, upper division, and graduate classes. UNC -Chapel Hill’s Department of History remains committed to rigorous archival research and publishing. Additionally, we have undertaken a few initiatives intended to increase the range of perspectives and experiences within our unit and the larger field. These include recruitment and mentoring programs for prospective graduate students, building new programs to train current graduate students for multiple career paths, including public facing scholarship. Applicants must demonstrate evidence of excellence in teaching and research. Candidates who have experience working with a diverse range of people, and who can contribute to a climate of inclusivity, are especially welcome. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit The Department of History is one of the largest academic departments within the College of Arts and Sciences. The Department is committed to the purposes of a research university, to the teaching of history at the undergraduate level as a core discipline in a liberal arts education, and to the training of graduate students. Position Summary Primary duties: Faculty member will be responsible for teaching a 2:2 load while achieving scholarly excellence in their own research, participating in departmental service, and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students. The Department of History at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in the history of science and technology. Responsibilities include teaching undergraduate and graduate students at both the introductory level as well as upper-level courses in areas of scholarly focus. Scholars who work on any chronological period and geographical specialization are invited to apply. We welcome applications from candidates whose work aligns with our departmental mission as outlined on our website (https://history.unc.edu/about-us/). Applicants must have PhD in hand in the field of history or a closely related field by July 1, 2027. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in History or related field and evidence of excellence in teaching and scholarship. Teaching load of two courses per semester; salary competitive and commensurate with qualifications. The appointment begins July 1, 2027. An offer of employment is contingent on a satisfactory pre-employment background check. Applicants should submit online at http://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/ 1) a cover letter; 2) a CV; and 3) the names of four references with contact information. The review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements Ph.D. in History or related fields to the history of science and technology and evidence of excellence in historical research, teaching and academic service. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience N/A Special Physical/Mental Requirements N/A Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions HOW TO APPLY : Qualified persons should submit all application materials: 1. Cover letter including descriptions of your current and future research, as well as your teaching experience and philosophy. 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. The names and contact information of FOUR references should be appended to the cover letter. (References will be contacted by the search committee when the short list of applicants is being determined.) Search Chair: Dr. Benjamin Waterhouse (waterhou@email.unc.edu) Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/325731 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Benjamin C Waterhouse, Professor Department Contact Telephone or Email waterhou@email.unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What is your highest degree/degree level? Masters PharmaD DNP PhD JD MLS/MIS DVM DDS MD Other Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326557": {
      "title": "Division Chief of Pediatric Cardiology",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Division Chief of Pediatric Cardiology Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Division Chief of Pediatric Cardiology Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Information Posting Information Department Peds-Cardiology - 412003 Posting Open Date 06/19/2026 Application Deadline Open Until Filled Yes Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Division Chief of Pediatric Cardiology Appointment Type Open Rank Vacancy ID FAC0005998 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Dependent on Experience and Qualifications Proposed Start Date 01/01/2027 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! A global higher education leader in innovative teaching, research and public service, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill consistently ranks as one of the nation’s top public universities . Known for its beautiful campus, world-class medical care, commitment to the arts and top athletic programs, Carolina is an ideal place to teach, work and learn. One of the best college towns and best places to live in the United States, Chapel Hill has diverse social, cultural, recreation and professional opportunities that span the campus and community. University employees can choose from a wide range of professional training opportunities for career growth, skill development and lifelong learning and enjoy exclusive perks for numerous retail, restaurant and performing arts discounts, savings on local child care centers and special rates on select campus events. UNC -Chapel Hill offers full-time employees a comprehensive benefits package , paid leave, and a variety of health, life and retirement plans and additional programs that support a healthy work/life balance. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit Our mission is to improve the health and well-being of North Carolinians and others whom we serve. We accomplish this by providing leadership and excellence in the interrelated areas of patient care, education and research. Patient care: We promote health and provide superb clinical care while maintaining our strong tradition of reaching underserved populations and reducing health disparities across North Carolina and beyond. Education: We prepare tomorrow’s healthcare professionals and biomedical researchers from all backgrounds by facilitating learning within innovative and integrated curricula and team-oriented interprofessional education to ensure a highly skilled workforce. Research: We develop and support a rich array of outstanding health sciences research programs, centers and resources. We provide infrastructure and opportunities for collaboration among disciplines throughout and beyond our university to support outstanding research. We foster programs in the areas of basic, translational, mechanistic and population research. Position Summary The Department of Pediatrics at UNC Children’s is recruiting an open-rank, open-track (fixed term, tenure/tenure-track, or variable track) faculty member, who will serve as Division Chief of Pediatric Cardiology to lead and expand the division’s clinical, educational, and research missions. The division provides comprehensive pediatric and congenital cardiology services across the continuum of care, supports a strong culture of clinical excellence and quality improvement, and serves as a central educational resource for the Department of Pediatrics and UNC Children’s Hospital. The Division Chief oversees all clinical operations, including inpatient and outpatient pediatric cardiology services, advanced cardiac imaging, cardiac catheterization and interventional programs, heart failure and transplant services, fetal cardiology, and care for patients with congenital heart disease across the lifespan. The division plays a critical role in multidisciplinary care of children with complex cardiac conditions and collaborates closely with cardiothoracic surgery, critical care, and other subspecialties. This leader will join at an exciting time for child health within the UNC Health system as well as in the state of North Carolina. This past year the Children’s Service Line was launched at UNC Health, with the goal of creating systems of care that will improve child health outcomes across the entire UNC Health system, statewide. In addition, planning is underway for North Carolina Children’s Hospital, the first freestanding children’s hospital in the state of North Carolina. This joint effort with Duke Children’s will include a large tertiary/quaternary Congenital Heart program. This Division Chief will play a major role, along with Cardiac and CT surgical leaders at UNC Children’s and Duke Children’s in the organization and launch of this combined program. The Raleigh-Durham-Chapel Hill area of North Carolina is an attractive place to live and work, with a great climate, schools, and both mountains and beaches within a day’s drive. UNC Health has a statewide series of hospitals and clinics and is affiliated with one of the nation’s leading medical schools. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements MD, DO, or equivalent. Board certified in Pediatrics and sub-board certification in Pediatric Cardiology. Past or current leadership experience within an academic Pediatric Cardiology division. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Demonstrated experience providing and supervising clinical care for pediatric patients with congenital and acquired heart disease, including management in a cardiac intensive care unit, cardiology step-down or intermediate care settings, and participation in multidisciplinary cardiac care teams. Strong leadership, effective interpersonal skills, team-building abilities, and clear communication. Special Physical/Mental Requirements Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/321118 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Jeff Anderson, Clinical Professor Department Contact Telephone or Email Jeff.Anderson@unchealth.unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Optional Documents Cover Letter Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What is your highest degree? PhD MD PharmD DDS DVM JD MLS/MIS Masters Other Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326545": {
      "title": "Post-Doc Research Associate - Wolberg Lab",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Post-Doc Research Associate - Wolberg Lab Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Post-Doc Research Associate - Wolberg Lab Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Cover Letter should address candidate's motivation for a postdoctoral position in the Wolberg laboratory. Candidate should submit a 0.5 -1-page statement on how their scientific experience and interest intersects with research in the Wolberg laboratory under the \"Other Documents\". For information on UNC Postdoctoral Benefits and Services click here Posting Information Posting Details Department Pathology Lab Med - Research - 413804 Posting Open Date 01/20/2026 Application Deadline Open Until Filled Yes Position Type Postdoctoral Scholar Position Title Post-Doc Research Associate - Wolberg Lab Vacancy ID PDS004755 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Temporary Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Work Location Chapel Hill, NC Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Dependent on Experience and Qualifications Proposed Start Date Estimated Duration of Appointment 12 Months Position Information Be a Tar Heel! A global higher education leader in innovative teaching, research and public service, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill consistently ranks as one of the nation’s top public universities and is among is the top ten research universities in the nation for federal research expenditures as well as for federally funded social and behavioral sciences research and development. Here at Carolina, our highly skilled postdocs play a vital role in our research enterprise and towards our overall commitment to research excellence. Across many disciplines, postdocs contribute to the intellectual vitality of the University. They provide innovative ideas and perspectives, foster a stimulating research environment and advance knowledge within their fields. Postdocs are crucial members of our scientific research workforce, contributors to our research outputs and an important reason why Carolina is one of the leading public research institutions in the country. UNC -Chapel Hill offers postdocs comprehensive medical and vision coverage , paid leave, and benefits and services that support professional development and a healthy work/life balance. Chapel Hill regularly ranks as one of the best college towns and best places to live in the United States, a reputation guided by the diverse social, cultural, recreation and professional opportunities that span the campus and community. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit Our mission is to improve the health and well-being of North Carolinians and others whom we serve. We accomplish this by providing leadership and excellence in the interrelated areas of patient care, education and research. Patient care: We promote health and provide superb clinical care while maintaining our strong tradition of reaching underserved populations and reducing health disparities across North Carolina and beyond. Education: We prepare tomorrow`s healthcare professionals and biomedical researchers from all backgrounds by facilitating learning within innovative and integrated curricula and team-oriented interprofessional education to ensure a highly skilled workforce. Research: We develop and support a rich array of outstanding health sciences research programs, centers and resources. We provide infrastructure and opportunities for collaboration among disciplines throughout and beyond our university to support outstanding research. We foster programs in the areas of basic, translational, mechanistic and population research. Position Summary Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death and disability in the Western world, and blood coagulation abnormalities contribute to the pathophysiology of nearly every disease, including cancer, infection, and autoimmune and inflammatory disorders. An NIH -funded postdoctoral position is available in the Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine in the laboratory of Dr. Alisa Wolberg at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill. The Wolberg Lab is part of UNC’s internationally recognized Blood Research Center. Research in the lab focuses on cellular, biochemical, and biophysical mechanisms that regulate blood function and contribute to health and disease. The successful applicant will advance ongoing R01-funded research focused on mechanisms of hemostasis and thrombosis. Potential areas of emphasis include factor (F) XIII biology (including regulation of FXIII -A and FXIII -B subunits, roles in venous thrombosis and thrombus resolution, and therapeutic targeting strategies); fibrin(ogen) degradation and the cellular and enzymatic drivers of thrombus resolution beyond plasminogen; and cellular and plasma mediators of FXI biology that influence bleeding risk. Additional opportunities exist in women’s health and blood disorders and mechanisms contributing to pulmonary embolism. Projects will be tailored to the fellow’s expertise, with the expectation that the postdoc will initially contribute to established research efforts and, over time, develop an independent line of investigation leading to future fellowship or career development funding. Candidates with experience in biochemistry or enzymology, cell biology (including tissue culture), histology, mouse models of thrombosis and hemostasis, and/or bioinformatics are encouraged to apply. The Wolberg Lab provides a highly supportive, team-oriented mentoring environment with strong emphasis on professional development, grant writing, and opportunities to mentor trainees, while fostering independence and ownership of scientific direction. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements PhD or MD in a biological science or a related field. Candidate must have previous research experience in a scientific laboratory setting. Required Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Candidate must have strong background in biological science and prior peer-reviewed publications documenting scientific project leadership with skills in experimental design, data analysis, data presentation, contextualization, and writing. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Experience in blood coagulation research is strongly preferred but not required. Candidates with experience in biochemistry or enzymology, cell biology including tissue culture, histology, mouse models of thrombosis and hemostasis, and/or bioinformatics are encouraged to apply. Special Physical/Mental Requirements Special Instructions Cover Letter should address candidate’s motivation for a postdoctoral position in the Wolberg laboratory. Candidate should submit a 0.5 -1-page statement on how their scientific experience and interest intersects with research in the Wolberg laboratory under the “Other Documents”. For information on UNC Postdoctoral Benefits and Services click here Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/312029 Posting Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Alisa Wolberg Department Contact Telephone or Email alisa_wolberg@med.unc.edu Postdoctoral Affairs Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Office of Postdoctoral Affairs at (919) 962-9982 or send an email to opahr@unc.edu . Please note: The Office of Postdoctoral Affairs will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Optional and Required Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter Other Document List of References Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Are you able to serve as a mandatory employee and report to work in support of mandatory University operations even when the University is closed? Yes No * Have you previously published your research results in a peer reviewed scientific journal? Yes, as Lead Author Yes, but not as Lead Author No * Do you have the ability to work with animals? Yes No Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326540": {
      "title": "Distinguished Professor",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Distinguished Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Distinguished Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. At the time of application, candidates will be required to identify the names, titles, and email addresses of four professional references. Recommenders identified by the applicant will be contacted via the application portal with instructions for uploading their letters of support for finalists only. Proposed appointment would begin on 1/1/2028, but an earlier start date is negotiable. Posting Information Posting Information Department Mathematics - 318900 Posting Open Date 10/06/2026 Application Deadline Open Until Filled Yes Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Distinguished Professor Appointment Type Tenured/Tenure Track Vacancy ID FAC0006150 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Dependent on Experience and/or Qualifications Proposed Start Date 01/01/2028 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! Applications received by November 16, 2026 will receive full consideration. Review of applications will continue on a rolling basis until the position is filled. A global higher education leader in innovative teaching, research and public service, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill consistently ranks as one of the nation’s top public universities . Known for its beautiful campus, world-class medical care, commitment to the arts and top athletic programs, Carolina is an ideal place to teach, work and learn. One of the best college towns and best places to live in the United States, Chapel Hill has diverse social, cultural, recreation and professional opportunities that span the campus and community. University employees can choose from a wide range of professional training opportunities for career growth, skill development and lifelong learning and enjoy exclusive perks for numerous retail, restaurant and performing arts discounts, savings on local child care centers and special rates on select campus events. UNC -Chapel Hill offers full-time employees a comprehensive benefits package , paid leave, and a variety of health, life and retirement plans and additional programs that support a healthy work/life balance. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit The mission of the Department of Mathematics is to extend the frontiers of mathematical knowledge and the ways it is used in the real world, to disseminate that knowledge and to train the next generation of teachers and researchers, as well as those choosing careers with industry or government. The Department is committed to building and nourishing interactions with other units across the UNC campus and with academic and industrial partners in North Carolina’s Research Triangle region, throughout the State and across the Nation. The Department maintains a strong research program as well as a comprehensive educational program. An active, healthy research environment is essential for training graduate students and enlivens undergraduate teaching by bringing students into contact with new and relevant mathematics, fostering fundamental mathematical skills and appreciation, and encouraging students to become the next generation of researchers and educators. Position Summary The UNC -Chapel Hill Department of Mathematics invites applications for a tenured Distinguished Professor. The position will also carry a secondary appointment as department chair. The successful candidate will provide visionary academic and administrative leadership for the Department of Mathematics, helping to shape and advance its intellectual direction and strategic priorities. In the chair role, the candidate will work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and university leadership to strengthen the Department’s excellence in teaching, research, and service. Responsibilities will include leading and overseeing the Department, teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, supervising graduate students, participating in academic service, and maintaining a strong, independent research program. For further information on the Department, please visit our website at http://www.math.unc.edu Minimum Education and Experience Requirements PhD in Mathematics, or related field. The candidate must have scholarly achievement appropriate for a tenured appointment at the level of full professor. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Academic experience appropriate for appointment at the level of full professor, evidence of distinguished scholarship and excellence in teaching, and demonstrated leadership experience. Special Physical/Mental Requirements Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions At the time of application, candidates will be required to identify the names, titles, and email addresses of four professional references. Recommenders identified by the applicant will be contacted via the application portal with instructions for uploading their letters of support for finalists only. Proposed appointment would begin on 1/1/2028, but an earlier start date is negotiable. Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326516 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Wei You, Distinguished Professor Department Contact Telephone or Email wyou@unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter Teaching Philosophy/evaluations Research Statement Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What is your highest degree/degree level? Masters PharmaD DNP PhD JD MLS/MIS DVM DDS MD Other Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326527": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Professor",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Teaching Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Teaching Assistant Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applications will begin on November 1st and applicants are encouraged to submit by that date for consideration in the initial review cycle. Please submit: A cover letter summarizing your teaching experience and interests, including the areas above in which you could contribute. A curriculum vitae detailing all courses taught, including enrollments. A two-page statement of teaching philosophy and practices, ideally including discussion of pedagogical approaches to engaging students in high-enrollment university courses. Selected applicants will be invited to submit a short teaching video responding to a common introductory-computing prompt. Further instructions will be provided with the invitation. Videos will be evaluated for technical accuracy, clarity, and suitability for undergraduate learners. A subset of applicants will then be invited to a 30-minute Zoom interview. At this stage, candidates will be asked to provide two reference letters, including at least one addressing their teaching or potential as an educator, ideally based on direct observation. Letters must be signed, appear on institutional letterhead, and be addressed to the Teaching Assistant Professor Search Committee. Finalists will be invited to campus to give a 50-minute talk, interviews and meals with faculty and staff, and a student lunch. Evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as student evaluations from prior courses taught as instructor of record or teaching assistant, midterm surveys of current students, or teaching observation reports. Upload this material in \"Other Document\". Posting Information Posting Information Department Computer Science - 318400 Posting Open Date 10/05/2026 Application Deadline 12/03/2026 Open Until Filled No Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Teaching Assistant Professor Appointment Type Fixed Term Faculty Vacancy ID FAC0006148 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Dependent on Experience and/or Qualifications Proposed Start Date 07/01/2027 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! The Department of Computer Science at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill seeks applications from individuals with exceptional teaching credentials for a full-time, teaching-track position at the rank of Teaching Assistant Professor. Successful candidates will be prepared to teach a range of foundational undergraduate computer science courses.We are searching for a candidate who can contribute to foundational undergraduate teaching while also bringing expertise in one or more of the following areas: ethics and society, artificial intelligence, AI engineering, software engineering, systems, robotics, computer architecture, and verification.The successful candidate will use evidence-based pedagogical practices and instructional technologies to support effective teaching, including in high-enrollment courses. Responsibilities will include developing course materials and assessments; training, mentoring, and supervising undergraduate and graduate teaching assistants; and contributing to undergraduate curriculum development and departmental service. Opportunities to teach Honors courses and in study abroad programs will be available in accordance with the candidate’s skills and interests. Formal academic research is welcome but not requisite for teaching-track faculty. This is a permanent, state-appropriated faculty line with an initial three-year appointment and subsequent renewal terms of up to five years. The position offers a promotion path to Teaching Associate Professor and Teaching Professor. The anticipated start date is July 1st, 2027. The department is part of UNC -Chapel Hill’s College of Arts and Sciences and offers B.A. and B.S. programs in computer science with a planned concentration in AI. We support faculty, staff, and learner development in an inclusive and respectful environment. Teaching faculty are valued members of our community, receive equitable representation in governance, and are supported by undergraduate and graduate teaching assistants who share our commitment to student success and broadening participation in computer science. Learn more at https://cs.unc.edu. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit The Department of Computer Science at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill has a uniquely rich history of excellence and leadership in teaching and research. As the second-oldest freestanding computer science department in the nation and the third-largest undergraduate major at UNC -Chapel Hill, it now enrolls more than 1,000 undergraduate majors between its Bachelor’s of Science and Bachelor’s of Arts degree programs and employs eleven (11) teaching-track faculty, twelve (12) research faculty, and more than 35 tenured/tenure-track faculty. Housed within UNC -CH’s vibrant College of Arts and Sciences, it serves an undergraduate population with broad talents and interests across the disciplines. Our Department strives to support faculty, staff, and learner development in an inclusive and respectful environment, and teaching faculty are valued members of our community. They receive equitable representation in governance and are well supported by undergraduate and graduate teaching assistants who share our commitment to fostering student success and broadening participation in computer science. For more information about the Department of Computer Science, please visit https://cs.unc.edu. Our Department strives to support faculty, staff, and learner development in an inclusive and respectful environment, and teaching faculty are valued members of our community. They receive equitable representation in governance and are well supported by undergraduate and graduate teaching assistants who share our commitment to fostering student success and broadening participation in computer science. For more information about the Department of Computer Science, please visit https://cs.unc.edu. Position Summary This is a full-time, teaching-track position at the rank of Teaching Assistant Professor. Successful candidates will be prepared to teach a range of foundational computer science and/or artificial intelligence courses. Opportunities to teach upper-division courses, Honors courses, and in study abroad programs will be available in accordance with each candidate’s skills and interests. Ideal candidates will be able to employ evidence-based pedagogical methods and instructional technologies to effectively teach large classes; train, utilize, and mentor undergraduate and graduate teaching assistants; and participate in the development of undergraduate curricula. Engagement in formal academic research is welcome but optional for teaching-track faculty. This position is a state-appropriated, permanent faculty line with an initial term of three years, to be renewed thereafter for terms of up to five years. It offers a promotion path to Teaching Associate Professor and Teaching Professor. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements Candidates must hold at least a Master’s degree in Computer Science or a closely related field before the start date of the position and have instructor-of-record experience or a minimum of two (2) semesters of academic teaching assistantship experience. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Candidates should have demonstrable experience with evidence-based pedagogical practices. Experience teaching large courses and employing evidence-based instructional interventions, innovations, and/or technologies to teach at scale is desired. Special Physical/Mental Requirements Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions Review of applications will begin on November 1st and applicants are encouraged to submit by that date for consideration in the initial review cycle. Please submit: A cover letter summarizing your teaching experience and interests, including the areas above in which you could contribute. A curriculum vitae detailing all courses taught, including enrollments. A two-page statement of teaching philosophy and practices, ideally including discussion of pedagogical approaches to engaging students in high-enrollment university courses. Selected applicants will be invited to submit a short teaching video responding to a common introductory-computing prompt. Further instructions will be provided with the invitation. Videos will be evaluated for technical accuracy, clarity, and suitability for undergraduate learners. A subset of applicants will then be invited to a 30-minute Zoom interview. At this stage, candidates will be asked to provide two reference letters, including at least one addressing their teaching or potential as an educator, ideally based on direct observation. Letters must be signed, appear on institutional letterhead, and be addressed to the Teaching Assistant Professor Search Committee. Finalists will be invited to campus to give a 50-minute talk, interviews and meals with faculty and staff, and a student lunch. Evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as student evaluations from prior courses taught as instructor of record or teaching assistant, midterm surveys of current students, or teaching observation reports. Upload this material in “Other Document”. Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326199 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Kris Jordan, Professor of the Practice Department Contact Telephone or Email kris@cs.unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter Other Document Certification/licenses Teaching Philosophy/evaluations Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What is your highest degree/degree level? Masters PharmaD DNP PhD JD MLS/MIS DVM DDS MD Other Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326517": {
      "title": "Post-Doc Research Associate",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Post-Doc Research Associate Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Post-Doc Research Associate Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. For information on UNC Postdoctoral Benefits and Services click here Posting Information Posting Details Department Applied Physical Sciences - 318100 Posting Open Date 10/05/2026 Application Deadline 10/19/2026 Open Until Filled No Position Type Postdoctoral Scholar Position Title Post-Doc Research Associate Vacancy ID PDS004991 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Temporary Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Work Location Chapel Hill, NC Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Dependent on Experience and/or Qualifications Proposed Start Date 01/01/2027 Estimated Duration of Appointment 12 Months Position Information Be a Tar Heel! A global higher education leader in innovative teaching, research and public service, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill consistently ranks as one of the nation’s top public universities and is among is the top ten research universities in the nation for federal research expenditures as well as for federally funded social and behavioral sciences research and development. Here at Carolina, our highly skilled postdocs play a vital role in our research enterprise and towards our overall commitment to research excellence. Across many disciplines, postdocs contribute to the intellectual vitality of the University. They provide innovative ideas and perspectives, foster a stimulating research environment and advance knowledge within their fields. Postdocs are crucial members of our scientific research workforce, contributors to our research outputs and an important reason why Carolina is one of the leading public research institutions in the country. UNC -Chapel Hill offers postdocs comprehensive medical and vision coverage , paid leave, and benefits and services that support professional development and a healthy work/life balance. Chapel Hill regularly ranks as one of the best college towns and best places to live in the United States, a reputation guided by the diverse social, cultural, recreation and professional opportunities that span the campus and community. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit Launched in 2013, the Department of Applied Physical Sciences ( APS ) is the first new science department in the College of Arts and Sciences in nearly 40 years. APS lives in the collaborative space between science and engineering, combining knowledge and discovery with an engineering mindset, team-based science, and entrepreneurship to address real-world problems. Organized around ‘Ideas to Impacts,’ the department unites with campus themes in health, water and energy to help Carolina continue to be a leader in science innovation and the application of knowledge. APS has united numerous scientific entities on campus to further its mission of finding interdisciplinary solutions to real-world problems. Its newest endeavor, BeAM (Be A Maker), is a university-wide initiative that incorporates Making into research, education, and entrepreneurship. BeAM is comprised of several spaces, including a makerspace in Hanes Art Center, Carmichael Residence Hall, and a 3,400 square foot Makerspace in Murray Hall, all of which serve the entire UNC community. Position Summary This Postdoctoral Research Associate will lead engineering work in the Bai lab’s translational research program on wearable optical sensing. A central effort is a multispectral pulse oximetry technology that measures blood oxygen saturation accurately across the full range of skin tones. The goal is to advance it from a laboratory prototype toward a clinically validated, manufacturable device. The postdoc will: Design, build, calibrate, and maintain wearable multi-wavelength optical sensors, including the optical front end, embedded electronics, and firmware. Develop and validate signal-processing and machine-learning algorithms that account for skin pigmentation, low perfusion, and motion. Work with UNC clinical collaborators on IRB -approved human-subjects validation studies, including device deployment, data quality control, and statistical analysis. Collaborate with industry partners on design for manufacture, design documentation, and regulatory strategy. Prepare technical reports, manuscripts, and invention disclosures, contribute to grant proposals, and mentor graduate and undergraduate researchers. The position offers direct experience with the full path from academic research to medical device translation. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements Ph.D. (or foreign equivalent) in biomedical engineering, electrical engineering, applied physics, materials science, computer science, or a closely related field, completed by the start date of the appointment. Required Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Hands-on experience designing, building, and characterizing optical or optoelectronic sensing systems (for example photoplethysmography, pulse oximetry, tissue spectroscopy, or wearable sensors). Proficiency in physiological or sensor signal processing and statistical or machine-learning modeling in Python or MATLAB . A record of first-author, peer-reviewed publications in a relevant field. Strong written and oral communication skills. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaborate with clinicians, engineers, and external partners. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Experience with embedded electronics, firmware, PCB design, or low-power wireless systems. Experience collecting and analyzing data from human-subjects or preclinical studies, including work in clinical settings under IRB and HIPAA requirements. Familiarity with pulse oximeter accuracy testing ( ISO 80601-2-61 and FDA guidance) and objective skin-tone measurement. Experience with medical device development under design controls and risk management ( ISO 13485, ISO 14971, IEC 60601). Background in tissue or skin optics. Exposure to technology commercialization (for example NSF I-Corps or invention disclosures). Experience mentoring students. Special Physical/Mental Requirements Special Instructions For information on UNC Postdoctoral Benefits and Services click here Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326287 Posting Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Wubin Bai, Associate Professor Department Contact Telephone or Email wbai@unc.edu Postdoctoral Affairs Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Office of Postdoctoral Affairs at (919) 962-9982 or send an email to opahr@unc.edu . Please note: The Office of Postdoctoral Affairs will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Optional and Required Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326514": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Sport Administration",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Assistant Professor - Sport Administration Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Assistant Professor - Sport Administration Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The application should include the following: 1) a cover letter, 2) a statement of previous and future research interests (3-page maximum), 3) a statement describing teaching methods commonly used and strategies for meeting the needs of students with a variety of learning styles (1 page maximum) and 4) a curriculum vitae. At the time of application, candidates will be required to identify the names, titles, and email addresses of four professional references who may be contacted by the search committee during the search process. For additional information, please contact the search committee chair Barbara Osborne at sportlaw@unc.edu . Deadline: Review of applications will begin on Nov. 15, 2026 . Zoom interviews will likely begin in the first few weeks of December, and on-campus interviews will begin in January. Position will close on December 18, 2026. Posting Information Posting Information Department Exercise and Sport Science - 318600 Posting Open Date 10/05/2026 Application Deadline 12/18/2026 Open Until Filled No Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Assistant Professor - Sport Administration Appointment Type Tenured/Tenure Track Vacancy ID FAC0006147 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Salary commensurate with experience Proposed Start Date 07/01/2027 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! A global higher education leader in innovative teaching, research and public service, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill consistently ranks as one of the nation’s top public universities . Known for its beautiful campus, world-class medical care, commitment to the arts and top athletic programs, Carolina is an ideal place to teach, work and learn. One of the best college towns and best places to live in the United States, Chapel Hill has diverse social, cultural, recreation and professional opportunities that span the campus and community. University employees can choose from a wide range of professional training opportunities for career growth, skill development and lifelong learning and enjoy exclusive perks for numerous retail, restaurant and performing arts discounts, savings on local child care centers and special rates on select campus events. UNC -Chapel Hill offers full-time employees a comprehensive benefits package , paid leave, and a variety of health, life and retirement plans and additional programs that support a healthy work/life balance. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill is a major research university in the University of North Carolina System with student enrollment exceeding 29,000 students. The Department of Exercise and Sport Science (exss.unc.edu) supports an undergraduate program of over 1,400 undergraduate majors with three specialization areas (Exercise and Sport Science, Fitness Professional, Sport Administration); a Master of Arts graduate program enrolling ~60 students also with three specializations (Exercise Physiology, Kinesiology, Sport Administration); and a doctoral program in Human Movement Science in partnership with the UNC -CH School of Medicine (hmsc.unc.edu). The mission of the Department of Exercise and Sport Science ( EXSS ) is to discover, create and promote knowledge of human movement to improve the quality of life of individuals and society. The mission of EXSS is further enhanced through interdisciplinary cooperation and departmental faculty securing competitive extramural funding in the form of contracts and grants. These endeavors enable the department to advance knowledge and thus serve the state and nation. The teaching, research, and service efforts of the EXSS Department are supported by an outstanding faculty and staff, which currently includes: 39 full-time faculty, 9 part-time (fixed term) faculty, 20 joint appointment/ adjunct faculty, 8 emeritus faculty, and 27 full-time EXSS staff members. Position Summary The Department of Exercise and Sport Science at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill invites applications for a 9-month tenure track Assistant Professor faculty position to deepen and expand research and teaching in Sport Administration. We aspire to recruit a faculty colleague who will engage in innovative and impactful scholarship that will diversify and enhance our research, teaching, and public outreach missions, and who can translate this research to teaching undergraduate and graduate level courses in sport law and other sport administration courses. Responsibilities: * Establish a focused line of research, publish articles in top-tier, peer-reviewed journals at a rate commensurate with an R1 institution, and seek intramural/extramural funding to support research. * Teach graduate and/or undergraduate courses (2/1 load) in sport administration including sport law and at least one other core undergraduate curriculum course (sport administration, sport economics/finance, sport marketing, sport facility and event management, sport revenue generation) or at least one graduate-level course ( NCAA governance and compliance, legal issues in college athletics, intercollegiate athletics administration, marketing college sport, college facility and event management, economics and finance in college athletics, research methods, and statistics). * Serve as a research advisor / thesis committee member for undergraduate and master’s students in sport administration. * Participate in departmental and university service and governance functions. * Perform other duties as assigned by the department chair. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements * A terminal degree (e.g. PhD, EdD) in sport administration/management or a closely related field. All degree requirements must be completed before the position start date. * Ability to teach sport law and at least one other core course in the sport administration curriculum. * Demonstrated ability to advise undergraduate independent study and/or graduate student research. * Demonstrated commitment to, or record of, student professional development and learning success. * Demonstrated ability to produce research publications in a clearly defined area of sport administration * Demonstrated record of, or potential for, securing external research funding (demonstrated fundable research interests). * Demonstrated interest in developing impactful collaborative opportunities within the Department, across the College of Arts and Sciences, and/or across the greater UNC community. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience * A juris doctorate (preferred) * Collegiate level teaching experience in sport law is preferred. * Experience working within the sport industry is preferred. Special Physical/Mental Requirements Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions The application should include the following: 1) a cover letter, 2) a statement of previous and future research interests (3-page maximum), 3) a statement describing teaching methods commonly used and strategies for meeting the needs of students with a variety of learning styles (1 page maximum) and 4) a curriculum vitae. At the time of application, candidates will be required to identify the names, titles, and email addresses of four professional references who may be contacted by the search committee during the search process. For additional information, please contact the search committee chair Barbara Osborne at sportlaw@unc.edu . Deadline: Review of applications will begin on Nov. 15, 2026 . Zoom interviews will likely begin in the first few weeks of December, and on-campus interviews will begin in January. Position will close on December 18, 2026. Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326414 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Barbara Osborne Department Contact Telephone or Email sportlaw@unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter List of References Teaching Philosophy/evaluations Research Statement Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What is your highest degree? PhD MD PharmD DDS DVM JD MLS/MIS Masters Other Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/326492": {
      "title": "Open Rank",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Open Rank Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Open Rank Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications should be submitted electronically through the following website: https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/323620 Applications should include a cover letter describing their qualifications and interest in the position, a curriculum vitae, and contact information for at least three professional references. Posting Information Posting Information Department Epidemiology - 463501 Posting Open Date 08/07/2026 Application Deadline Open Until Filled Yes Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Open Rank Appointment Type Open Rank Vacancy ID FAC0006049 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Proposed Start Date 09/01/2027 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! A global higher education leader in innovative teaching, research and public service, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill consistently ranks as one of the nation’s top public universities . Known for its beautiful campus, world-class medical care, commitment to the arts and top athletic programs, Carolina is an ideal place to teach, work and learn. One of the best college towns and best places to live in the United States, Chapel Hill has diverse social, cultural, recreation and professional opportunities that span the campus and community. University employees can choose from a wide range of professional training opportunities for career growth, skill development and lifelong learning and enjoy exclusive perks for numerous retail, restaurant and performing arts discounts, savings on local child care centers and special rates on select campus events. UNC -Chapel Hill offers full-time employees a comprehensive benefits package , paid leave, and a variety of health, life and retirement plans and additional programs that support a healthy work/life balance. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit The Department of Epidemiology (http://www.sph.unc.edu/epid/) is internationally recognized as a leader in epidemiologic research and training. The Department of Epidemiology is one of the eight departments located in the Gillings School of Global Public Health, the number one ranked public school of Public Health in the US. The achievements of our faculty, staff, students, and alumni contribute to the department’s standing among the leading academic departments of epidemiology in the world. The department has approximately 37 Tenured- Tenure Track Faculty, 13 Joint Faculty, 19 Fixed Term Faculty, 129 Adjunct Faculty, 52 staff, and approximately 20 Postdoctoral Fellows and 230 students (150 PhD, 10 MSCR , 70 MPH ). The department’s operating budget was $35 million in sponsored funding across research, training, and public service activities in 2025. Position Summary Application review will begin September 15 and continue until positions are filled. One or two full-time tenure-track faculty positions in the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor (open rank) in the Department of Epidemiology. Competitive candidates will demonstrate strong capacity for research that impacts public health, advances the field of epidemiology, and contributes to the department’s teaching mission. As members of the Department of Epidemiology at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, faculty are expected to develop independently funded research portfolios, contribute impactful scholarly products, mentor master’s and doctoral students, and teach core epidemiology methods and/or substantive area courses in epidemiology. The department’s primary focus is the development and application of research methods to understand the patterns and causes of health and disease, and the application of that knowledge to improving the health of populations. The department provides strong training in research methods and opportunities to apply epidemiology to a wide range of health problems. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements PhD or equivalent degree in Epidemiology or a closely related field prepared to conduct independent research, contribute high-quality teaching, and mentor student training in epidemiology research. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Tenure-track faculty at all levels are expected to demonstrate excellence in teaching, service and scholarship through their scholarly contributions to science, capacity to obtain extramural funding that supports independent research, classroom instruction, and mentoring master’s and doctoral students. Candidates for the rank of Associate Professor must additionally demonstrate research leadership through funded epidemiologic research (in the role of PI/ MPI role on grants or contracts) and a track record for training graduate students (demonstrated through both mentoring and teaching). Candidates for the rank of Full Professor must further demonstrate excellence in scholarship with national or international recognition, research leadership with sustained research funding (in the role of PI/ MPI on grants or contracts). Special Physical/Mental Requirements Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions Applications should be submitted electronically through the following website: https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/323620 Applications should include a cover letter describing their qualifications and interest in the position, a curriculum vitae, and contact information for at least three professional references. Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/323620 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Sharon Sullivan, HR Consultant Department Contact Telephone or Email sharon_sullivan@unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter List of References Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). What is your highest degree? PhD MD PharmD DDS DVM JD MLS/MIS Masters Other Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
