    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/325511": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Assistant Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The application should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae (that explicitly states fields of specialization and grounding in economic and workforce development planning), statements of research and teaching interests, 1 representative paper, graduate transcript and a list of four professional references. Four professional references need to be identified by the applicant and their contact information (names, titles, email addresses) should be provided. Referees should be able to comment on the applicants teaching ability. At the time of application, candidates will be required to identify the names, titles, and email addresses of four professional references. For a limited selection of applicants, recommenders identified by the applicant will be contacted via the application portal with instructions for uploading their letters of support. We will begin considering candidates on October 1. For questions related to the application process, please contact the department at DCRP_HR@unc.edu; the department address is Department of City and Regional Planning, University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, CB# 3140, Chapel Hill, NC 27599-3140 and the web address is https://planning.unc.edu/ Posting Information Posting Information Department City and Regional Planning - 315600 Posting Open Date 09/16/2026 Application Deadline 11/05/2026 Open Until Filled No Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Assistant Professor Appointment Type Tenured/Tenure Track Vacancy ID FAC0006098 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Dependent on Experience and/or Qualifications Proposed Start Date 07/01/2027 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! A global higher education leader in innovative teaching, research and public service, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill consistently ranks as one of the nation’s top public universities . Known for its beautiful campus, world-class medical care, commitment to the arts and top athletic programs, Carolina is an ideal place to teach, work and learn. One of the best college towns and best places to live in the United States, Chapel Hill has diverse social, cultural, recreation and professional opportunities that span the campus and community. University employees can choose from a wide range of professional training opportunities for career growth, skill development and lifelong learning and enjoy exclusive perks for numerous retail, restaurant and performing arts discounts, savings on local child care centers and special rates on select campus events. UNC -Chapel Hill offers full-time employees a comprehensive benefits package , paid leave, and a variety of health, life and retirement plans and additional programs that support a healthy work/life balance. Economies work well when everyone can participate, prosper, and reach their full potential. For this to happen, planners need to understand how communities, businesses, workers, institutional actors, technologies and regulatory structures interact within in local economies and wider spheres to create shared prosperity and economic benefits for all. A well-resourced economy is also well positioned to drive new innovations in support of healthier, resilient and more environmentally sustainable communities. The Department of City & Regional Planning has long been a national leader in economic development and has strong connections with public administration and business administration programs within the university. Strong historical connections exist with applied economic centers on campus and economic organizations across the state and beyond, such as NC Growth, the Development Finance Initiative, The World Bank, IADB , WRI , Oxfam, the Ford Foundation, International Economic Development Council, NC Department of Commerce, Self-Help Credit Union, the NC Justice Center, NC Economic Development Association and several local economic development organizations. The department seeks scholars who can nurture and expand these connections. We seek an emerging scholar with strong conceptual and quantitative grounding in economic and workforce development planning. The candidate should have an active research program, ideally but not exclusively, at the intersection of jobs, technological change, organization of work, and innovative institutional structures that shape these processes and are shaped by them. The research should contribute to supporting vibrant, just, and resilient local and regional economies. In addition to research, the candidates are expected to teach core courses in the specialization such as Economic Development Techniques (quantitative methods) and Planning for Jobs among others. They are also expected to contribute to the curriculum by teaching First Year Seminars for undergraduates and developing courses in workforce development that are attractive to students in the department and allied fields. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit The Department of City and Regional Planning is an academic unit within the College of Arts and Sciences. The Department engages in teaching, primarily at the graduate level; conducts applied research on urban and regional issues; and serves the state, nation, and planning profession. The department offers a Masters in City and Regional Planning, a PhD, and an undergraduate minor. In any given year there are about 85 Masters students enrolled, 35 doctoral students and a growing cadre of undergraduates and students enrolled in dual degree programs. The faculty consists of 22 core faculty consisting of tenure-track faculty members and full-time teaching faculty along with part-time practitioners and researchers all of whom are active in the teaching and research programs of the department. The faculty usually generates over $3.0 million annually through research and training grants. Position Summary The tenure-track Assistant Professor will allocate effort approximately as follows: 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. The position carries a two-course teaching load each semester during the fall and spring terms. Teaching assignments will be based on the candidate’s expertise and departmental needs. The position will conduct rigorous research using appropriate methodologies, including a variety of quantitative and/or qualitative methods. The Assistant Professor will conduct service that includes, but is not limited to search committees, review committees and administrative roles within the department and/or College. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements Ph.D. in Planning or related fields. (Ph.D. should be received by the start date of the appointment (July 1, 2027). Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Demonstrated commitment to teaching, mentoring, and training students at all levels and to work with a diverse student body; strong research and teaching records; ability to develop or extend or record of scholarly contributions. Special Physical/Mental Requirements Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions The application should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae (that explicitly states fields of specialization and grounding in economic and workforce development planning), statements of research and teaching interests, 1 representative paper, graduate transcript and a list of four professional references. Four professional references need to be identified by the applicant and their contact information (names, titles, email addresses) should be provided. Referees should be able to comment on the applicants teaching ability. At the time of application, candidates will be required to identify the names, titles, and email addresses of four professional references. For a limited selection of applicants, recommenders identified by the applicant will be contacted via the application portal with instructions for uploading their letters of support. We will begin considering candidates on October 1. For questions related to the application process, please contact the department at DCRP_HR@unc.edu; the department address is Department of City and Regional Planning, University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, CB# 3140, Chapel Hill, NC 27599-3140 and the web address is https://planning.unc.edu/ Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/325475 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Diana Devereaux, Dept Manager Department Contact Telephone or Email DCRP_HR@unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter Other Document List of References Writing/research Sample Teaching Philosophy/evaluations Research Statement Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What is your highest degree/degree level? Masters PharmaD DNP PhD JD MLS/MIS DVM DDS MD Other Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/325510": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Professor",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Teaching Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Teaching Assistant Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The application should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae (CV) that explicitly states fields of specialization, teaching philosophy statement, student teaching evaluations, and a list of references that includes the names, titles and email addresses of three professional references. Application materials should address the applicants experience, commitment to, and approach to teaching at the undergraduate level and/or the applicants experience training large groups in professional contexts. Additional optional materials may be included in the application packet, such as evidence of teaching effectiveness, a writing sample or publication related to pedagogical practice, or documentation demonstrating public engagement, professional leadership, or community-based training activities. At the time of application, candidates will also be required to identify the names, titles, and email addresses of three professional references. Recommenders identified by the applicant will be contacted via the application portal with instructions for uploading their letters of support. Reference letters should speak to the applicants teaching effectiveness and instructional abilities, as well as relevant experience such as professional training delivery, AICP or APA involvement/certification, and/or experience as a practicing planner where applicable. We will begin considering candidates on October 1. For questions related to the application process, please contact the department at DCRP_HR@unc.edu; the department address is Department of City and Regional Planning, University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, CB# 3140, Chapel Hill, NC 27599-3140 and the web address is Home - Carolina Planning Posting Information Posting Information Department City and Regional Planning - 315600 Posting Open Date 09/16/2026 Application Deadline 11/03/2026 Open Until Filled No Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Teaching Assistant Professor Appointment Type Fixed Term Faculty Vacancy ID FAC0006097 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Dependent on Experience/Qualifications Proposed Start Date 07/01/2027 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! A global higher education leader in innovative teaching, research and public service, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill consistently ranks as one of the nation’s top public universities . Known for its beautiful campus, world-class medical care, commitment to the arts and top athletic programs, Carolina is an ideal place to teach, work and learn. One of the best college towns and best places to live in the United States, Chapel Hill has diverse social, cultural, recreation and professional opportunities that span the campus and community. University employees can choose from a wide range of professional training opportunities for career growth, skill development and lifelong learning and enjoy exclusive perks for numerous retail, restaurant and performing arts discounts, savings on local child care centers and special rates on select campus events. UNC -Chapel Hill offers full-time employees a comprehensive benefits package , paid leave, and a variety of health, life and retirement plans and additional programs that support a healthy work/life balance. The Department of City and Regional Planning at UNC -Chapel Hill is seeking a new Teaching Assistant Professor to expand the Department’s intellectual reach and visibility to UNC’s undergraduate student population. This position will also facilitate recruitment of undergraduates into the Master’s of City and Regional Planning ( MCRP ) program through the Department’s “4+1” undergraduate-to-graduate pathway, creating a structured “on-ramp” for students to discover planning earlier in their academic trajectory. This position will strengthen the Department’s instructional capacity by introducing undergraduates to the practice of planning. The selected faculty member will accomplish this through three primary pathways: leading (and creating new) large introductory courses, expanding the capacity of the Department’s undergraduate Minor in Urban Studies and Planning, and creating new, innovative undergraduate courses (including both large lecture courses and small, first-year seminars). By leading introductory (100- and 200-level) urban planning courses, this faculty member will serve as a key gateway into the discipline, introducing undergraduates majoring in a variety of fields to planning’s role in housing and community development, environmental protection, economic development and infrastructure provision. As these courses will be students’ first exposure to planning, a strong, engaging instructor in this role will significantly shape perceptions of the field and broaden its appeal beyond traditional self-selected majors. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit The Department of City and Regional Planning is an academic unit within the College of Arts and Sciences. The Department engages in teaching, primarily at the graduate level; conducts applied research on urban and regional issues; and serves the state, nation, and planning profession. The department offers a Masters in City and Regional Planning, a PhD, and an undergraduate minor. In any given year there are about 85 Masters students enrolled, 35 doctoral students and a growing cadre of undergraduates and students enrolled in dual degree programs. The faculty consists of 22 core faculty consisting of tenure-track faculty members and full-time teaching faculty along with part-time practitioners and researchers all of whom are active in the teaching and research programs of the department. The faculty usually generates over $3.0 million annually through research and training grants. Position Summary This full-time Teaching Assistant Professor will be expected to allocate effort approximately as follows: 80% teaching and 20% service. This service includes, but is not limited to, search committees, review committees, and administrative roles within the department and/or College. The position carries a three-course teaching load each semester during the fall and spring terms. Teaching assignments will be based on the candidate’s expertise and departmental needs. The six courses taught by this Teaching Assistant Professor will be at the undergraduate level and include a mix of existing and new courses. This position will be hired with a 3-year initial contract with the possibility of renewal and promotion, in accordance with university and departmental personnel policies. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements A master’s degree in urban planning or a related discipline, should be received by the start date of the appointment (July 1, 2027). Demonstrated teaching experience at the undergraduate level, or experience delivering professional training or educational programming to large groups is expected. Must demonstrate strong evidence of competency and expertise in urban planning. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience A PhD in urban planning or a related discipline is preferred. Candidates should have a record of, or demonstrated potential for, outstanding teaching at the undergraduate level. Alternatively, candidates with experience as practicing planners should demonstrate extensive experience in leading professional training or other activities that promote planning education for large groups. Professional credentials, such as AICP certification, and active engagement with professional organizations, such as the American Planning Association ( APA ), are desirable. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to teaching, mentoring, and training undergraduate students and to working with a diverse student body. Special Physical/Mental Requirements Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions The application should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae (CV) that explicitly states fields of specialization, teaching philosophy statement, student teaching evaluations, and a list of references that includes the names, titles and email addresses of three professional references. Application materials should address the applicants experience, commitment to, and approach to teaching at the undergraduate level and/or the applicants experience training large groups in professional contexts. Additional optional materials may be included in the application packet, such as evidence of teaching effectiveness, a writing sample or publication related to pedagogical practice, or documentation demonstrating public engagement, professional leadership, or community-based training activities. At the time of application, candidates will also be required to identify the names, titles, and email addresses of three professional references. Recommenders identified by the applicant will be contacted via the application portal with instructions for uploading their letters of support. Reference letters should speak to the applicants teaching effectiveness and instructional abilities, as well as relevant experience such as professional training delivery, AICP or APA involvement/certification, and/or experience as a practicing planner where applicable. We will begin considering candidates on October 1. For questions related to the application process, please contact the department at DCRP_HR@unc.edu; the department address is Department of City and Regional Planning, University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, CB# 3140, Chapel Hill, NC 27599-3140 and the web address is Home – Carolina Planning Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/325486 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Diana Devereaux, Dept Manager Department Contact Telephone or Email DCRP_HR@unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae / Resume Cover Letter List of References Teaching Philosophy/evaluations Optional Documents Other Document Writing/research Sample Other Document 2 Other Document 3 Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * What is your highest degree/degree level? Masters PharmaD DNP PhD JD MLS/MIS DVM DDS MD Other Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/325497": {
      "title": "Transfusion Medicine Pathologist",
      "description": "UNC-Chapel Hill Employment Opportunities | Transfusion Medicine Pathologist Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Careers At Carolina Notifications Log In /Create Account Help Working at Carolina Transfusion Medicine Pathologist Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please include names and addresses of four (4) references when uploading the required List of References document. Posting Information Posting Information Department Pathology Lab Med - Clinical - 413802 Posting Open Date 06/12/2025 Application Deadline Open Until Filled Yes Position Type Permanent Faculty Working Title Transfusion Medicine Pathologist Appointment Type Faculty Vacancy ID FAC0005550 Full-time/Part-time Full-Time Permanent Hours per week 40 FTE 1 Position Location North Carolina, US Hiring Range Proposed Start Date 03/01/2026 Position Information Be a Tar Heel! A global higher education leader in innovative teaching, research and public service, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill consistently ranks as one of the nation’s top public universities . Known for its beautiful campus, world-class medical care, commitment to the arts and top athletic programs, Carolina is an ideal place to teach, work and learn. One of the best college towns and best places to live in the United States, Chapel Hill has diverse social, cultural, recreation and professional opportunities that span the campus and community. University employees can choose from a wide range of professional training opportunities for career growth, skill development and lifelong learning and enjoy exclusive perks for numerous retail, restaurant and performing arts discounts, savings on local child care centers and special rates on select campus events. UNC -Chapel Hill offers full-time employees a comprehensive benefits package , paid leave, and a variety of health, life and retirement plans and additional programs that support a healthy work/life balance. Primary Purpose of Organizational Unit Our mission is to improve the health and well-being of North Carolinians and others whom we serve. We accomplish this by providing leadership and excellence in the interrelated areas of patient care, education and research. Patient care: We promote health and provide superb clinical care while maintaining our strong tradition of reaching underserved populations and reducing health disparities across North Carolina and beyond. Education: We prepare tomorrow`s healthcare professionals and biomedical researchers from all backgrounds by facilitating learning within innovative and integrated curricula and team-oriented interprofessional education to ensure a highly skilled workforce. Research: We develop and support a rich array of outstanding health sciences research programs, centers and resources. We provide infrastructure and opportunities for collaboration among disciplines throughout and beyond our university to support outstanding research. We foster programs in the areas of basic, translational, mechanistic and population research. Position Summary The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine is seeking a full-time physician for a tenure-track/tenured, fixed-term, or variable track position at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor for the Transfusion Medicine Service. Our Transfusion Medicine Service encompasses donor collections, therapeutic apheresis, hematopoietic progenitor cell collection and processing, blood component processing, and compatibility testing. Applicants with secondary interests in some other area of the pathology lab, such as surgical pathology, hematopathology, or clinical pathology, are welcome to apply. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements Candidates must hold a MD/DO degree, be BC/BE in Transfusion Medicine, and must have or be eligible for a North Carolina medical license. Preferred Qualifications, Competencies, and Experience Applicants should be committed to excellence in clinical services, teaching and research in a field relevant to transfusion medicine. Expertise in clinical coagulation is a particular need. Special Physical/Mental Requirements Campus Security Authority Responsibilities Not Applicable. Special Instructions Please include names and addresses of four (4) references when uploading the required List of References document. Quick Link https://unc.peopleadmin.com/postings/302543 Department Contact Information Department Contact Name and Title Matthew Karafin, Chair of the Search Committee Department Contact Telephone or Email matthew.karafin@unchealth.unc.edu Academic Personnel Office Contact Information If you experience any problems accessing the system or have questions about the application process, please contact the University’s Executive Vice Chancellor and Provost office at (919)-962-1091 or send an email to facultyrecruitment@unc.edu. Please note: The Executive Vice Chancellor & Provost office will not be able to provide specific updates regarding position or application status. If you have any questions about the job requirements or the hiring department notify the Department Contact. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement The University is an equal opportunity employer and welcomes all to apply without regard to age, color, gender, gender expression, gender identity, genetic information, national origin, race, religion, sex, or sexual orientation. We encourage all qualified applicants to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae / Resume List of References Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Office of Human Resources 919-843-2300 hr@unc.edu © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill About Human Resources UNC-Chapel Hill Policies Office of Faculty Affairs To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
